# Activity 1: Individual Project Snapshot

## Title

**AlgoMon Loop**

## One-sentence Idea

A 2D cyber monster-battler roguelite where the player hacks through a procedural data network, captures algorithmic creatures, and uses tactical turn-based battles to become stronger.

## Player Action

The player repeatedly chooses paths on a node network, enters battles, selects Attack / Status / Defense skills, manages HP and CP, captures or upgrades AlgoMons, and builds a stronger team.

## Unity Format

2D game; top-down / node-map style for exploration, with side-view tactical battle screens.

## Vertical Slice

A playable slice will include one small node-map route, one enemy encounter, one complete turn-based battle, and a simple win/lose result screen.

The player should be able to choose a path, fight with one AlgoMon, use Attack / Status / Defense skills, manage CP, and finish the battle.

## Core Systems

The first systems I need are:

- Node-map selection system
- AlgoMon data system using ScriptableObjects
- Skill and CP system
- Turn-order priority queue
- ASD counter system
- Battle manager
- Basic battle UI for HP, CP, skill buttons, and feedback text

## GitHub Status

Repository already created:

[https://github.com/JiachiZhu/Game-programming--AlgoMon-Loop.git](https://github.com/JiachiZhu/Game-programming--AlgoMon-Loop.git)

## Biggest Risk

The biggest risk is that the battle system may become too complex because it includes turn priority, CP costs, elemental matchups, and Attack / Status / Defense counters.

To reduce this risk, I will build the smallest playable battle first, then add extra skills, status effects, and roguelite systems later.

## Next Action

Before the next session, I will create a visible playable battle prototype in Unity with one player AlgoMon, one enemy AlgoMon, HP/CP displays, and basic Attack / Status / Defense buttons.

## Peer Feedback

The feedback I received was that animation may be difficult to produce, sprite balance may be hard to manage, and networking / multiplayer would be challenging to implement.

Based on this feedback, I will keep the first version focused on single-player gameplay, use simple sprite animations first, and avoid multiplayer until the core battle loop is working.
